%% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% CAPTION
%%
%% What this shows: the project is built by two people who deliberately do not
%% wait on each other. Karl (Track E) writes code against synthetic stand-in
%% data; Isaac (Track G) supplies the real data and the science parameters.
%% They meet only at the seven frozen contract files in the middle, and at
%% numbered integration checkpoints. That is why the engine already runs
%% end-to-end even though most of the real data has not arrived.
%%
%% Why it matters: everything in red is blocked on Isaac, and nothing in Track
%% E can unblock it. The four outstanding deliverables are the GEBCO
%% bathymetry grid, the TS-6 surface digitization, the real Dryad chamber
%% dataset, and the blank GEOLOGY-ISAAC parameters (the GRADE join tolerance,
%% the mean-nodule-mass source, the real study-area bounds, and the economic
%% cutoffs). Two of the seven contracts are fully delivered, two are partly
%% filled, and three are still placeholders.
%%
%% The next meeting point is Integration Checkpoint 1, which swaps the
%% synthetic DEM for real bathymetry. Nothing else changes at that checkpoint
%% -- the eight terrain recipes simply recompute on real terrain, which is the
%% whole point of building against a contract.
